.SH NAME
BIO_printf, BIO_vprintf, BIO_snprintf, BIO_vsnprintf
\&\- formatted output to a BIO
.SH SYNOPSIS
.IX Header "SYNOPSIS"
.Vb 1
\& #include <openssl/bio.h>
\&
\& int BIO_printf(BIO *bio, const char *format, ...);
\& int BIO_vprintf(BIO *bio, const char *format, va_list args);
.Ve
.PP
Deprecated since OpenSSL 4.1, can be hidden entirely by defining
\&\fBOPENSSL_API_COMPAT\fR with a suitable version value, see
\&\fBopenssl_user_macros\fR\|(7):
.PP
.Vb 2
\& int BIO_snprintf(char *buf, size_t n, const char *format, ...);
\& int BIO_vsnprintf(char *buf, size_t n, const char *format, va_list args);
.Ve
.SH DESCRIPTION
.IX Header "DESCRIPTION"
\&\fBBIO_printf()\fR is similar to the standard C \fBprintf()\fR function, except that
the output is sent to the specified BIO, \fIbio\fR, rather than standard
output.  All common format specifiers are supported.
.PP
\&\fBBIO_vprintf()\fR is similar to the \fBvprintf()\fR function found on many platforms,
the output is sent to the specified BIO, \fIbio\fR, rather than standard
output.  All common format specifiers are supported. The argument
list \fIargs\fR is a stdarg argument list.
.PP
\&\fBBIO_snprintf()\fR and \fBBIO_vsnprintf()\fR were provided when not every supported
platform shipped a C99 \fBsnprintf()\fR implementation. That is no longer the
case: every supported build target provides C99 \fBsnprintf()\fR, and new code
should use the standard library functions directly. \fBBIO_snprintf()\fR and
\&\fBBIO_vsnprintf()\fR are retained for source compatibility but are deprecated
as of OpenSSL 4.1.
.PP
\&\fBBIO_snprintf()\fR and \fBBIO_vsnprintf()\fR have the same signatures as \fBsnprintf()\fR
and \fBvsnprintf()\fR but they do not have the same return value on truncation.
When the formatted output would exceed \fIn\fR bytes, \fBsnprintf()\fR returns the
number of bytes that would have been written had the buffer been large
enough, while \fBBIO_snprintf()\fR and \fBBIO_vsnprintf()\fR return \-1. Because the
functions look identical at the call site, callers reach for the standard
C99 idiom by reflex, and the divergence becomes a source of bugs. New
code should call \fBsnprintf()\fR and \fBvsnprintf()\fR directly.
.SH "RETURN VALUES"
.IX Header "RETURN VALUES"
\&\fBBIO_printf()\fR and \fBBIO_vprintf()\fR return the number of bytes written, or
\&\-1 on error.
.PP
\&\fBBIO_snprintf()\fR and \fBBIO_vsnprintf()\fR return the number of bytes written to
\&\fIbuf\fR (excluding the terminating \f(CW\*(Aq\e0\*(Aq\fR) on success, or \-1 if the
formatted output would not fit in \fIn\fR bytes or on other error.
.SH NOTES
.IX Header "NOTES"
When \fIn\fR is greater than 0, \fBBIO_snprintf()\fR and \fBBIO_vsnprintf()\fR always
terminate \fIbuf\fR with \f(CW\*(Aq\e0\*(Aq\fR, including in the truncation case where \-1
is returned.
.SH HISTORY
.IX Header "HISTORY"
\&\fBBIO_snprintf()\fR and \fBBIO_vsnprintf()\fR were deprecated in OpenSSL 4.1. Callers
should use the standard C library functions \fBsnprintf()\fR and \fBvsnprintf()\fR
instead.
